--- short ---
Missing Hours is a science-fiction screenplay set inside a closed virtual-reality match that looks like downtown Bennington, Vermont, in 1984. The players, who enter as vampires and cannot log out, must feed on the town's inhabitants, who are artificial minds that believe themselves real. Sleep erases each inhabitant's last waking hours. The only exits are elimination or a dawn ritual at one stone for a single prize. Hale, a calculating player, maps the town and feeds three night workers short of death, intending to collect the ritual tokens and leave unmarked. Cora Voss, a graveyard waitress, photographs empty rooms and records radio songs as analog proof she can keep. A rival researcher stacks hypnotic lies on Hale's three workers, and the nightly erasure fails. Cora wakes with scraps of night, matches a bruise to a nurse, and her Polaroids catch ritual residue. Hale uses her archive to lift tokens, then returns a print he should have kept. The diner locks, the nurse refuses him, and his rotation dies. She plays his feed instructions from a cassette. He starts a hypnotic overwrite for the last token and stops. At Founders' Stone he holds every token and knows speaking the invocation would erase every hour she has kept. He withholds the words, gives her the tokens and cassette, and takes a permanent sun-scorch. She hides the tokens so no player wins. The match stays running. He remains inside it. After sunrise she clips his developing Polaroid beside her tip jar.
