--- short ---
False Weight is a fantasy screenplay set in Highkettle, a late-iron-age mountain hamlet in a secondary world. Each midwinter the village must present a presentation-relic and an iron tithe to the valley Hold or lose pass-protection and winter grain. A forged relic holds the compact only while a living smith pays a hidden bodily levy that ends only with his death or the object's public breaking; the Hold does not test authenticity unless the piece is challenged. It follows Willa, a grown apprentice sent to recover the stolen relic.

Twenty-one days before midwinter, Willa and her master Orren weigh thin stock for the unfinished tithe. That night raiders steal the relic, leaving the iron. The hamlet sends Willa; Orren cannot cross the winter pass. She walks the pass, takes the relic from the sleeping camp by craft without killing, and returns frostbitten. Six days before the rite she reads a forty-year false weld. Orren admits he forged it and bound the levy to himself. Unable to settle her debt, she chooses silence, winter work, and leaving in spring. At midwinter the steward accepts the forged relic and thin tithe without an inner test. That night she names the weld as metal; he repeats the old reasons and is not forgiven. Next morning they work at an anvil that is no longer home; she sets a true bar and the arm levels. Orren keeps paying; the village keeps a year of grain, ignorant the protection dies with him.
